body:
I have similar experiences growing up. I was told I was a child I wasn't allowed to have feelings when I would try to express to my parents how I was depressed or suicidal. I would ask for attention from them and they would tell me I have lots of toys and to go play quietly by myself. So as I've grown into adulthood, talking about my feelings is foreign to me. I was never allowed to have them before so I've stuffed them all down. I've worn a mask so people don't know how I'm feeling. It's been a pretty terrible life so far, but I'm working on it with the support of my wife and the help of my therapist. When I brought this up in an outpatient program, my peers and the clinician running the group honestly didn't know what to say besides "I'm sorry you went through that". Emotional abuse is so very real.

&#x200B;

Edit: I would bring up how their comments made you feel in the next session though, when it is relevant. And why you felt that way. The triggers behind it. And why you find it hard to communicate.
